        {
          "path": "_m/u/1/7/3/museum-de-cruquius-wp/cruquius-gemaal.jpg",
          "filename": "Cruquius Gemaal.jpg",
          "caption": "The pumping stations got the names of famous hydraulic engineers: Leeghwater, Lijnden and Cruquius. The construction of the Leeghwater was started in 1845, the construction of the Lijnden and Cruquius began in 1847. \nFor the design of buildings in the Gothic Revival style drew the Dutch public works engineer in January Anne Beijerinck (1800-1874), while the machines were designed by Joseph Gibbs and Arthur Dean of London.\n\nThe steam engine itself was built by Harvey &amp; Co. Hayle in Cornwall), the pumps by Fox &amp; Co in Falmouth (Cornwall), and poor balance and steam boilers by Van Vlissingen &amp; Dudok van Heel in Amsterdam.",
          "alt": "The pumping stations got the names of famous hydraulic engineers: Leeghwater, Lijnden and Cruquius. The construction of the Leeghwater was started in 1845, the construction of the Lijnden and Cruquius began in 1847. \nFor the design of buildings in the Gothic Revival style drew the Dutch public works engineer in January Anne Beijerinck (1800-1874), while the machines were designed by Joseph Gibbs and Arthur Dean of London.\n\nThe steam engine itself was built by Harvey &amp; Co. Hayle in Cornwall), the pumps by Fox &amp; Co in Falmouth (Cornwall), and poor balance and steam boilers by Van Vlissingen &amp; Dudok van Heel in Amsterdam.",
          "width": 4647,
          "height": 3091,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "Bas",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cruquius_Gemaal.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
